view score of 6.28/10 against the 9.29 benchmark (not on track). Around 57.9% of the population has electricity access and 41.2% use the internet, with an AfDB AIDI 2024 score of 25.7.","narrative":"Nigeria has scale: Africa's largest population and arable base, a deep agri-fintech and commodity-exchange scene in Lagos, and active DFI money, including AfDB's $2.2bn phase-2 SAPZ mobilisation and a $200m NAGS-AP loan in 2026. On outcomes it lags. Federal agriculture spending was only about 1-2% of the budget over the last decade, and the 2026 allocation is about N1.45trn (~2.4-2.8%), against the 10% Maputo/Malabo pledge. Release and execution gaps make that worse. Insecurity across northern food belts (banditry, kidnapping, farmer-herder conflict), FX shocks and very low irrigation (~154,000 ha) push down output: maize fell from 12.9mt in 2022 to ~11mt in 2024. The programmes keep changing names (ATA, Anchor Borrowers, NAGS-AP, SAPZ) and implementation is slow; AfDB rated SAPZ unsatisfactory at 12% disbursement. Recent pluses are a NIN-linked national farmer registry, mechanisation and cooperative reforms, and reported Q2-2026 sector growth of 4.39%.","strengths":["Large domestic market and diversified agro-ecologies (cereals, roots and tubers, tree crops)","Vibrant private agri-tech, commodity-exchange and fintech ecosystem (AFEX, ThriveAgric, Lagos fintechs)","Strong DFI pipeline: AfDB-led SAPZ phase 2 across 24 states; NAGS-AP phase II loan","IITA headquarters in Ibadan plus the ARCN research network","Domestic fertiliser (urea) manufacturing capacity, e.g. Dangote and Indorama"],"flagship_programs":["National Agricultural Technology and Innovation Policy (NATIP 2022-2027)","Special Agro-Industrial Processing Zones (SAPZ)"],"atlas":{"indicators":{"account_pct":63.3,"ag_spend_pct_budget":2.4,"ag_va_per_worker_usd":3495,"caadp_br4":6.28,"cereal_yield_t_ha":1.56,"electricity_pct":57.9,"fertilizer_kg_ha":3.54,"internet_pct":41.2,"irrigated_pct_cropland":0.66},"n_pillar