g/wiki/List_of_countries_by_number_of_Internet_users"],"needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"iso3":"ZAF","name":"South Africa","region":"Southern","recs":["SADC"],"capital":"Pretoria","population_m":63.21,"ag_gdp_pct":2.9,"ag_jobs_pct":18.8,"electricity_access_pct":99.7,"internet_users_pct":78.4,"caadp_br4_score":null,"aidi_rank":5,"aidi_score":null,"deep":true,"summary":"Agriculture contributes about 2.9% of GDP and employs about 18.8% of workers, with a 4th CAADP Biennial Review score not captured in this dataset. Around 99.7% of the population has electricity access and 78.4% use the internet, with an AfDB AIDI 2024 rank of 5/54.","narrative":"South Africa is the continent's most commercialized and export-oriented agricultural sector (exports of about US$14bn in 2025). Its advantages are deep private capital, commercial banks with agri desks, strong organized agriculture (Agbiz, Agri SA, commodity bodies) and a capable research base (ARC, universities). The Agriculture and Agro-processing Master Plan (AAMP, launched May 2022) gives a social-compact framework, and stakeholders credit it with better coordination. Implementation remains stronger at the strategic level than on the ground, and access to blended finance for emerging farmers is still limited. Leadership churn has been significant. Willie Aucamp replaced John Steenhuisen as Minister of Agriculture on 30 June 2026, amid the country's largest-ever foot-and-mouth disease (FMD) outbreak, which has disrupted red-meat exports. Land Bank leadership has turned over repeatedly. The binding constraints are outside the farm gate: underperforming Transnet ports and rail, neglected rural roads, electricity reliability, and slow land-reform and title transfer. Public agriculture spending is a small share of the budget, well below 10%, but private investment does much of the work.","strengths":["Highly commercial, export-competitive sector (citrus, deciduous fruit, wine, grains) with sophisticated agribusiness and finance","AAMP social compact (2022) with public-private dialogue platforms","Strong research and agritech base: ARC, Stellenbosch and Pretoria universities, Aerobotics, Khula!","Organized commodity bodies and private extension and data","Deep commercial agri-finance (Absa AgriBusiness, Standard Bank, Land Bank)"],"flagship_programs":["Agriculture and Agro-processing Master Plan (AAMP)"],"atlas":{"indicators":{"account_pct":81.1,"ag_spend_pct_budget":1.6,"ag_va_per_worker_usd":10818,"caadp_br4":null,"cereal_yield_t_ha":4.56,"electricity_pct":99.7,"fertilizer_kg_ha":67.16,"internet_pct":78.4,"irrigated_pct_cropland":13.45},"n_pillars":5,"pillars":{"finance":94,"infrastructure":92,"inputs_water":84,"policy":16,"productivity":95},"score":76},"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_African_countries_by_population","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_GDP_sector_composition","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_sector_composition_of_the_labor_force","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_electrification_rate","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_number_of_Internet_users","https://africa.com/top-10-news/africas-infrastructure-race-the-10-countries-leading-the-charge","https://engineeringnews.co.za/article/da-reshuffles-gnu-team-aucamp-replaces-steenhuisen-as-agriculture-minister-2026-06-17","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minister_of_Agriculture_(South_Africa)","https://www.foodformzansi.co.za/aucamp-outlines-strategy-for-biosecurity-trade-and-infrastructure/","https://www.foodformzansi.co.za/three-years-on-is-the-master-plan-delivering-for-farmers/","https://www.thecommonsense.co.za/politics/ramaphosa-appoints-sihlobo-new-agriculture-envoy","https://www.engineeringnews.co.za/article/agbiz-elects-vkp-group-ceo-pg-strauss-as-chairperson-2026-06-04","https://www.engineeringnews.co.za/article/land-bank-appoints-jabu-mphambo-acting-ceo-2026-02-17","https://www.foodformzansi.co.za/arc-leader-envisions-resilient-tech-driven-farming-future/","https://www.gov.za/node/816933"],"needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"iso3":"ZMB","name":"Zambia","region":"Southern","recs":["COMESA","SADC"],"capital":"Lusaka","population_m":20.72,"ag_gdp_pct":1.8,"ag_jobs_pct":55.4,"electricity_access_pct":46.7,"internet_users_pct":17.1,"caadp_br4_score":null,"aidi_rank":null,"aidi_score":null,"deep":false,"summary":"Agriculture contributes about 1.8% of GDP and employs about 55.4% of workers, with a 4th CAADP Biennial Review score not captured in this dataset. Around 46.7% of the population has electricity access and 17.1% use the internet, with no AIDI 2024 score captured here.","narrative":null,"strengths":[],"flagship_programs":["Comprehensive Agriculture Transformation Support Programme (CATSP)","Lobito Corridor"],"atlas":{"indicators":{"account_pct":72.7,"ag_spend_pct_budget":1.1,"ag_va_per_worker_usd":304,"caadp_br4":null,"cereal_yield_t_ha":2.32,"electricity_pct":46.7,"fertilizer_kg_ha":76.48,"internet_pct":17.1,"irrigated_pct_cropland":4.06},"n_pillars":5,"pillars":{"finance":85,"infrastructure":20,"inputs_water":78,"policy":11,"productivity":45},"score":48},"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_African_countries_by_population","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_GDP_sector_composition","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_sector_composition_of_the_labor_force","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_electrification_rate","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_number_of_Internet_users"],"needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"iso3":"ZWE","name":"Zimbabwe","region":"Southern","recs":["COMESA","SADC"],"capital":"Harare","population_m":16.34,"ag_gdp_pct":5.4,"ag_jobs_pct":52.5,"electricity_access_pct":49,"internet_users_pct":41.6,"caadp_br4_score":5.45,"aidi_rank":null,"aidi_score":null,"deep":false,"summary":"Agriculture contributes about 5.4% of GDP and employs about 52.5% of workers, with a 4th CAADP Biennial Review score of 5.45/10 against the 9.29 benchmark (not on track). Around 49% of the population has electricity access and 41.6% use the internet, with no AIDI 2024 score captured here.","narrative":null,"strengths":[],"flagship_programs":["Agriculture Food Systems and Rural Development Strategy 2025-2030","Pfumvudza/Intwasa conservation farming"],"atlas":{"indicators":{"account_pct":49.5,"ag_spend_pct_budget":null,"ag_va_per_worker_usd":826,"caadp_br4":5.45,"cereal_yield_t_ha":1.07,"electricity_pct":49,"fertilizer_kg_ha":25.91,"internet_pct":41.6,"irrigated_pct_cropland":4.28},"n_pillars":5,"pillars":{"finance":49,"infrastructure":48,"inputs_water":67,"policy":59,"productivity":28},"score":50},"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_African_countries_by_population","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_GDP_sector_composition","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_sector_composition_of_the_labor_force","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_electrification_rate","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_number_of_Internet_users","https://esaff.org/index-php/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Policy-Brief-SADC-Performance-in-the-CAADP-BR-Report-wb.pdf"],"needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"}]$atlas$::jsonb)
on conflict (iso3) do update set name = excluded.name, region = excluded.region, recs = excluded.recs, capital = excluded.capital, population_m = excluded.population_m, ag_gdp_pct = excluded.ag_gdp_pct, ag_jobs_pct = excluded.ag_jobs_pct, electricity_access_pct = excluded.electricity_access_pct, internet_users_pct = excluded.internet_users_pct, caadp_br4_score = excluded.caadp_br4_score, aidi_rank = excluded.aidi_rank, aidi_score = excluded.aidi_score, deep = excluded.deep, summary = excluded.summary, narrative = excluded.narrative, strengths = excluded.strengths, flagship_programs = excluded.flagship_programs, atlas = excluded.atlas, sources = excluded.sources, needs_review = excluded.needs_review, review_note = excluded.review_note, last_verified = excluded.last_verified;

-- news: 20 rows
insert into news (id, date, headline, summary, why, countries, region, segment, type, source, url)
select id, date, headline, summary, why, countries, region, segment, type, source, url from jsonb_populate_recordset(null::news, $atlas$[{"id":"n-20261117-cameroon-ndop-rice","date":"2026-11-17","headline":"Cameroon earmarks CFA7 billion to widen Ndop rice plain cultivation","summary":"Cameroon's agriculture minister announced CFA7 billion to add more than 5,000 hectares of rice land in the Ndop valley under the PIISAH import-substitution plan. A new mill there can handle 20 tonnes of paddy an hour and could yield about 65,000 tonnes of milled rice a year, against 869,039 tonnes of rice imported in 2025.","why":"It shows the state backing land, mechanisation and milling together to cut rice imports, though the funding schedule has not been published.","countries":["CMR"],"region":"Central","segment":"processing","type":"policy","source":"Business in Cameroon","url":"https://www.businessincameroon.com/agriculture/1711-17201-cameroon-allocates-cfa7-billion-to-expand-ndop-rice-production-reduce-reliance-on-imports"},{"id":"n-20261117-douala-port-gas-plant","date":"2026-11-17","headline":"Douala port signs deal for 300 MW gas power plant","summary":"Douala Port Power Corporation signed with a Genertec CMC-led Chinese consortium for a 300 MW gas-fired plant at Douala-Bonabéri, valued at about CFAF628 billion (roughly $1.06 billion). The first phase covers a 102 MW unit and a gas pipeline of about 200 km from Kribi, under a 25-year build-operate-transfer concession.","why":"More reliable power at Central Africa's main port would help cold storage, agro-processing and export handling, but the lenders and gas supplier are still unnamed.","countries":["CMR"],"region":"Central","segment":"energy","type":"infrastructure","source":"Ecofin Agency","url":"https://www.ecofinagency.com/news-infrastructures/1711-60863-cameroon-douala-port-plans-300-megawatt-gas-plant-in-two-phases"},{"id":"n-20261116-burundi-ruzizi-irrigation","date":"2026-11-16","headline":"Burundi starts irrigation works on 1,735 hectares in Ruzizi Plain","summary":"Work has started to irrigate three sites totalling 1,735 hectares in western Burundi under a project led by the government and the AfDB, with IFAD support. The Burundi component is funded with $77.98 million in grants and aims to lift rice yields from 3.5 to 5.5 t/ha and reach about 120,000 rural households.","why":"It is a concrete grant-funded irrigation build-out in a fragile state and includes agro-processing hubs and rural roads.","countries":["BDI"],"region":"East","segment":"irrigation & water","type":"infrastructure","source":"allAfrica (AfDB release)","url":"https://allafrica.com/stories/202611170088.html"},{"id":"n-20261116-dangote-ghana-cocoa","date":"2026-11-16","headline":"Dangote in talks to process 500,000 tonnes of Ghanaian cocoa","summary":"Ghana's finance minister said Aliko Dangote has proposed processing 500,000 tonnes of cocoa locally, as the 2027 budget targets 50% domestic processing by 2028. Existing grinders already run at half capacity or less because they cannot get enough beans.","why":"A large new grinder would compete with existing plants for scarce beans, which could reshape local cocoa value addition and bean allocation.","countries":["GHA"],"region":"West","segment":"processing","type":"company","source":"Ecofin Agency","url":"https://www.ecofinagency.com/news-agriculture/1611-60832-dangote-eyes-ghana-cocoa-where-plants-already-struggle-to-find-beans"},{"id":"n-20261116-dutch-bird-flu-poultry-imports","date":"2026-11-16","headline":"Dutch avian flu outbreaks threaten poultry supply to five African importers","summary":"After new bird flu outbreaks in the Netherlands, Morocco suspended Dutch poultry imports on 11 November. Ghana, Zambia, Mozambique, DR Congo and Benin together took about 83% of the roughly 97,000 tonnes of Dutch poultry shipped to Africa in 2025, and Dutch product made up about 54% of Zambia's poultry imports.","why":"Import bans could tighten frozen poultry supply and raise prices, which opens room for local poultry producers.","countries":["GHA","ZMB","MOZ","COD","BEN","MAR"],"region":"Continental","segment":"logistics & market access","type":"market","source":"Ecofin Agency","url":"https://www.ecofinagency.com/news-agriculture/1611-60843-dutch-bird-flu-raises-supply-risks-for-five-major-african-poultry-importers"},{"id":"n-20261116-sa-agri-jobs-q3","date":"2026-11-16","headline":"South African farm employment falls by 34,000 in third quarter","summary":"Stats SA's labour survey shows agriculture lost 34,000 jobs between Q2 and Q3 2026, with primary farm employment down about 4% to 911,000. Economists attribute the drop to rising input costs, even as total national employment grew by 101,000.","why":"Farm job losses during a period of overall job growth point to cost pressure on producers, which matters for labour planning and for policy support.","countries":["ZAF"],"region":"Southern","segment":"inputs & seeds","type":"market","source":"Food For Mzansi","url":"https://www.foodformzansi.co.za/sa-agriculture-sheds-34-000-jobs-as-input-costs-squeeze-farmers/"},{"id":"n-20261116-tanzania-sgr-turkish-loan","date":"2026-11-16","headline":"Tanzania negotiates $280 million Turkish loan for railway sections","summary":"Tanzania is in final talks with Turkey's Exim Bank for $280 million to complete 533 km of its standard gauge railway between Makutupora, Tabora and Isaka. Separately, on 4 November the AfDB announced a $1.2 billion financing package for the Tabora–Kigoma section.","why":"The SGR is expected to cut freight costs from Dar es Salaam to inland and DRC markets, which would affect how farm inputs and produce move across the region.","countries":["TZA"],"region":"East","segment":"transport","type":"infrastructure","source":"Ecofin Agency","url":"https://www.ecofinagency.com/news-infrastructures/1611-60841-tanzania-seeks-280-million-turkish-loan-to-complete-key-railway-sections"},{"id":"n-20261115-ghana-dawa-solar-industrial","date":"2026-11-15","headline":"Ghana industrial-zone solar plant secures $18.15 million debt commitment","summary":"The Emerging Africa & Asia Infrastructure Fund committed $18.15 million to a 100 MWp solar plant at Dawa, part of a $60.6 million debt package alongside IFC and IDA. The $88.6 million project will serve more than 95 businesses in the Dawa and Tema industrial zones.","why":"Cheaper and steadier power for industrial zones lowers operating costs for food processors and agro-industries located there.","countries":["GHA"],"region":"West","segment":"industrial zones","type":"infrastructure","source":"Ecofin Agency","url":"https://www.ecofinagency.com/news-industry/1511-60824-ghana-based-100-mwp-solar-project-secures-18-15-million-to-power-industrial-zones"},{"id":"n-20261115-oil-price-food-costs","date":"2026-11-15","headline":"Higher oil prices raise food and transport costs for African importers","summary":"The World Bank expects energy prices to rise 32% in 2026, with Brent averaging $91 a barrel, and food commodity prices to rise about 10%. Fuel-importing African countries face tighter diesel supply and higher freight costs.","why":"Diesel prices feed directly into costs for mechanisation, irrigation pumping and haulage, so producers' margins will tighten.","countries":["AU"],"region":"Continental","segment":"energy","type":"market","source":"Ecofin Agency","url":"https://www.ecofinagency.com/news-industry/1511-60821-global-oil-price-surge-adds-to-food-and-transport-cost-pressures-in-african-fuel-importing-countries"},{"id":"n-20261114-sa-citrus-exports-2026","date":"2026-11-14","headline":"South African citrus exports slip to 201 million cartons","summary":"South Africa shipped 201 million cartons of citrus in 2026, down 1.5% on 2025 and 4% below the April forecast. Lemons grew 23% while navels fell 20%, and growers cite shipping disruption, Durban port backlogs, floods and a strong rand.","why":"Port and freight bottlenecks are now a main limit on export earnings for one of Africa's largest fresh-produce industries.","countries":["ZAF"],"region":"Southern","segment":"logistics & market access","type":"market","source":"Food For Mzansi","url":"https://www.foodformzansi.co.za/sa-citrus-exports-dip-to-201-million-cartons-after-bruising-season/"},{"id":"n-20261113-shea-production-data-gap","date":"2026-11-13","headline":"Shea sector lacks reliable production figures across 20-plus countries","summary":"Analysts say no one has a clear figure for how much shea is produced or collected across a savanna belt covering more than 20 countries. The last industry-wide report covered 2010–2020, and the planned 2021–2026 update has no funding yet. Côte d'Ivoire officially recorded 51,697 tonnes of kernels in 2025/26.","why":"Without basic supply data it is hard to plan processing investment, price kernels or design policy for a crop that matters to millions of rural women.","countries":["CIV","GHA","BFA","NGA","MLI"],"region":"West","segment":"data","type":"research","source":"Ecofin Agency","url":"https://www.ecofinagency.com/news/1311-60783-africa-s-shea-industry-flies-blind-on-how-much-it-produces"},{"id":"n-20261112-zimbabwe-idle-farm-levy","date":"2026-11-12","headline":"Zimbabwe lawmakers propose $50-per-hectare levy on idle farms","summary":"A parliamentary agriculture committee proposed a yearly US$50/ha charge on large farms left idle without good reason, rising each year the land stays unused. The revenue would be ring-fenced for irrigation, extension and rural infrastructure, with exemptions for drought or hardship.","why":"If adopted, it would change the cost of holding unused farmland and could speed up leasing or investment in under-used land.","countries":["ZWE"],"region":"Southern","segment":"policy","type":"policy","source":"Farmer's Weekly","url":"https://www.farmersweekly.co.za/agri-news/africa/zimbabwe-portfolio-committee-proposes-annual-levy-on-idle-farms/"},{"id":"n-20261111-african-avocado-exports-2026","date":"2026-11-11","headline":"African avocado exporters see uneven 2026 results","summary":"South Africa exported 21 million 4-kg cartons, up 16%. Kenya stopped sea-freight harvesting early from 30 October because too little fruit was export-ready. Morocco expects about 100,000 tonnes of exports for 2026/27, up from roughly 70,000 tonnes last season.","why":"The results show how weather, freight costs and quality rules shape returns for Africa's growing avocado export sector.","countries":["ZAF","KEN","MAR"],"region":"Continental","segment":"logistics & market access","type":"market","source":"Ecofin Agency","url":"https://www.ecofinagency.com/news-agriculture/1111-60709-africas-avocado-exporters-face-mixed-fortunes-in-2026-season"},{"id":"n-20261110-egypt-wheat-11mt","date":"2026-11-10","headline":"Egypt reports 11 million tonnes of wheat as farmland expands","summary":"Egypt's agriculture minister told the Senate that wheat output reached 11 million tonnes from 3.7 million feddans, with total farmland at 10.5 million feddans. Fertiliser subsidies exceeded EGP 35 billion, and farm exports reached 9.5 million tonnes.","why":"Higher domestic wheat output in Africa's largest wheat importer affects regional grain trade and shows what large input subsidies can deliver.","countries":["EGY"],"region":"North","segment":"inputs & seeds","type":"market","source":"Daily News Egypt","url":"https://dailynewsegypt.com/2026/11/10/egypts-wheat-output-reaches-11m-tonnes-agriculture-minister-tells-senate/"},{"id":"n-20261109-kifiya-ifc-investment","date":"2026-11-09","headline":"IFC invests in Ethiopian agri-lending platform Kifiya","summary":"The IFC made an undisclosed investment in Kifiya, whose AI credit-scoring system has supported more than $1 billion in lending. Its farmer-profiling project in Ethiopia has already registered more than 1.2 million farmers.","why":"Backing from a development finance institution for data-driven credit scoring could widen smallholder access to loans in markets where collateral is scarce.","countries":["ETH"],"region":"East","segment":"finance & insurance","type":"funding","source":"Disrupt Africa","url":"https://disruptafrica.com/2026/11/09/ethiopian-fintech-kifiya-secures-ifc-investment-to-scale-agricultural-lending/"},{"id":"n-20261109-morocco-tomato-export-curb","date":"2026-11-09","headline":"Morocco halts new tomato export permits for African markets","summary":"From 5 November, Morocco stopped issuing new tomato export permits for African destinations to lower domestic prices after production problems in Souss-Massa. Mauritania alone imported about 34,000 tonnes of Moroccan tomatoes in 2025, while European shipments are unaffected.","why":"The move shows how quickly export controls can cut supply to West African importers and create openings for their local growers.","countries":["MAR","MRT","SEN"],"region":"North","segment":"policy","type":"policy","source":"Ecofin Agency","url":"https://www.ecofinagency.com/news-agriculture/0911-60637-morocco-limits-tomato-exports-to-africa-as-domestic-prices-rise"},{"id":"n-20261105-koolboks-tlg-credit","date":"2026-11-05","headline":"Solar refrigeration firm Koolboks secures private credit from TLG Capital","summary":"Nigeria-based Koolboks received a credit facility of undisclosed size from TLG Capital, backed by a Cascador guarantee arranged with Morgan Stanley. The company has sold more than 15,000 solar freezers and operates in Nigeria, Kenya and Uganda.","why":"Debt finance for off-grid cooling can expand cold storage for traders and small food businesses who lack reliable power.","countries":["NGA","KEN","UGA"],"region":"West","segment":"storage & cold chain","type":"funding","source":"Nairametrics","url":"https://nairametrics.com/2026/11/05/koolboks-secures-tlg-capital-funding-to-expand-solar-cooling-across-africa/"},{"id":"n-20261104-afdb-tanzania-tadb-equity","date":"2026-11-04","headline":"AfDB lends $60 million to recapitalise Tanzania's agricultural development bank","summary":"The AfDB approved a $60 million loan, which Tanzania will invest as equity in the Tanzania Agricultural Development Bank, and the government will add a matching $60 million. Agriculture employs about 65% of Tanzanians but receives only about 12% of private credit.","why":"A larger capital base should let TADB lend more to farmers and agribusinesses and help narrow an estimated $1.5 billion annual financing gap.","countries":["TZA"],"region":"East","segment":"finance & insurance","type":"funding","source":"allAfrica (AfDB release)","url":"https://allafrica.com/stories/202611050699.html"},{"id":"n-20261104-au-ifrc-zero-hunger","date":"2026-11-04","headline":"AU Commission chair calls for agrifood overhaul at zero-hunger meeting","summary":"At an AU–IFRC meeting on ending hunger, AU Commission chair Mahmoud Ali Youssouf called for climate-resilient farming, more intra-African trade and the use of technology such as AI to raise farm productivity. He presented the CAADP 2026–2035 plan as the framework for this work.","why":"It signals continental priorities under the new CAADP Kampala agenda, which governments and donors use to align their agriculture spending.","countries":["AU"],"region":"Continental","segment":"policy","type":"policy","source":"APO Group / Africa Newsroom (AU release)","url":"https://www.africa-newsroom.com/press/advancing-africas-path-towards-zero-hunger-through-stronger-partnerships?lang=en"},{"id":"n-20261104-wami-agro-follow-on","date":"2026-11-04","headline":"Ghanaian agritech Wami Agro gets follow-on backing from Sahara, Acumen","summary":"Sahara Impact Ventures and Acumen provided follow-on grant funding of undisclosed size to Wami Agro, which supplies inputs on credit, repaid at harvest, to more than 60,000 smallholders. The company plans a processing facility and aims to reach 100,000 farmers by 2027.","why":"Input credit repaid at harvest, tied to buyer links, is a model several Ghanaian agritechs are now scaling.","countries":["GHA"],"region":"West","segment":"finance & insurance","type":"funding","source":"Dealroom News","url":"https://dealroom.co/news/169766-sahara-impact-ventures-acumen-back-ghanas-wami-agro-in-follow-on-grant/"}]$atlas$::jsonb)
on conflict (id) do update set date = excluded.date, headline = excluded.headline, summary = excluded.summary, why = excluded.why, countries = excluded.countries, region = excluded.region, segment = excluded.segment, type = excluded.type, source = excluded.source, url = excluded.url;

-- benchmarks: 24 rows
insert into benchmarks (id, source_country, theme, what_happened, outcome, transferability, link, needs_review, review_note, last_verified)
select id, source_country, theme, what_happened, outcome, transferability, link, needs_review, review_note, last_verified from jsonb_populate_recordset(null::benchmarks, $atlas$[{"id":"green-revolution-hyv-seeds-fertilizer-irrigation-credit-pric","source_country":"India","theme":"Green Revolution (HYV seeds + fertilizer + irrigation + credit + price support)","what_happened":"From the mid-1960s India bundled Mexican/Philippine HYV wheat and rice with assured irrigation, subsidized fertilizer, farm credit and procurement prices, starting in Punjab, Haryana and western UP.","outcome":"HYV area grew from 1.9m ha (FY1960) to 15.4m (1970), 43.1m (1980) and 63.9m ha (1990). By FY1980, 75% of wheat area and 45% of rice area used HYVs. Gains concentrated where water was assured; poverty fell faster in high-yield states.","transferability":"High for the input bundle and sequencing (start in high-potential irrigated zones). Medium overall, because Africa's crop diversity, rain-fed systems and soil heterogeneity need many crop-specific varieties, and the groundwater depletion and fertilizer-subsidy fiscal burden should be avoided.","link":"https://countrystudies.us/india/104.htm","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"digital-public-infrastructure-upi-india-stack","source_country":"India","theme":"Digital public infrastructure: UPI (India Stack)","what_happened":"Interoperable real-time payment rails (UPI, 2016) on top of Aadhaar ID and Jan Dhan accounts, run by NPCI as a public-private utility with zero merchant fees.","outcome":"24.07bn transactions worth Rs 29.37 lakh crore in Sept 2026 (~802m a day); 218.6bn transactions in FY26. Rails enable direct benefit transfers and digital credit for farmers.","transferability":"High: Africa already leads in mobile money. The gap is interoperability and open ID/registries; PAPSS and national instant-payment systems (Ethiopia, Nigeria NIP, Ghana GhIPSS) can copy the open-API model.","link":"https://startuptalky.com/india-upi-transactions-september-2026/","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"pm-kisan-direct-benefit-transfer-to-farmers","source_country":"India","theme":"PM-KISAN direct benefit transfer to farmers","what_happened":"Since 2019, unconditional Rs 6,000/yr income support paid in three installments by DBT to Aadhaar-seeded bank accounts.","outcome":"Over Rs 4.27 lakh crore transferred to 9.32 crore (93m) farmers, including 2.15 crore women, by March 2026 (22nd installment Rs 18,640 crore).","transferability":"Medium: delivery rails (ID + registry + payments) are highly transferable for e-voucher input subsidies (Nigeria, Zambia e-FISP, Malawi). Universal cash at this scale is fiscally hard for most African budgets.","link":"https://ianslive.in/total-transfer-to-farmers-under-pm-kisan-scheme-shoots-past-427-lakh-crore--20260319130304","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"e-nam-national-electronic-agricultural-market","source_country":"India","theme":"e-NAM national electronic agricultural market","what_happened":"Launched 2016 to link physical APMC mandis on an online trading platform with e-auctions, assaying and online payment.","outcome":"1,656 mandis integrated, 1.80 crore farmers, 2.73 lakh traders and 4,724 FPOs registered; cumulative trade of 13.25 crore t worth Rs 4.84 lakh crore over 10 years (to Mar 2026). Inter-state trade adoption has stayed modest.","transferability":"Medium: commodity exchanges (Ethiopia ECX, Ghana GCX, Malawi AHCX) show demand, but success depends on assaying, warehousing and trader buy-in. Within AfCFTA, regional e-markets could link surplus and deficit zones.","link":"https://www.lokmattimes.com/business/digital-agri-push-e-nam-integrates-1656-mandis-facilitates-rs-484-lakh-crore-trade","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"digital-agriculture-mission-agristack-farmer-id-crop-survey-","source_country":"India","theme":"Digital Agriculture Mission / AgriStack (farmer ID, crop survey, geo-referenced land registry)","what_happened":"Launched 2024: federated farmer registry linked to land records, digital crop survey and a geospatial village map. Used for credit, insurance, procurement and advisory.","outcome":"11 crore (110m) farmer IDs generated by Oct 2026, five months ahead of the March 2027 deadline.","transferability":"High: the clearest template for African farmer registries (Kenya KIAMIS, Ethiopia, Rwanda, Nigeria). Requires land-record digitization and data protection consistent with the AU Data Policy Framework.","link":"https://swarajyamag.com/news-brief/digital-agriculture-mission-crosses-11-crore-farmer-ids-five-months-before-march-2027-deadline","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"embrapa-and-the-cerrado-transformation","source_country":"Brazil","theme":"Embrapa and the Cerrado transformation","what_happened":"Embrapa (founded 1973) and universities developed liming and soil correction, tropical soybean varieties, biological N-fixation and no-till for the acidic Cerrado savanna. Combined with credit and infrastructure, this opened the Midwest to double-cropping.","outcome":"Agricultural TFP grew ~3.08%/yr (1975-2016) and explained 80.6% of output growth. Output rose 3.82%/yr vs 0.72% for inputs (more than 4x output in 40 years). Crop area went from 36.8m to 69.5m ha while pasture shrank. Embrapa's budget was R$3.4bn in 2017.","transferability":"High for Africa's acidic savanna zones and for a well-funded, autonomous national research corporation. Watch out for deforestation and land-concentration trade-offs.","link":"https://www.choicesmagazine.org/choices-magazine/theme-articles/the-agricultural-production-potential-of-latin-american-implications-for-global-food-supply-and-trade/brazils-agricultural-production-and-its-potential-as-global-food-supplier","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"plano-safra-annual-subsidized-rural-credit-plan","source_country":"Brazil","theme":"Plano Safra: annual subsidized rural credit plan","what_happened":"A predictable annual plan sets volumes and interest-rate tiers for rural credit (operating, commercialization, investment), with separate lines for family farmers (Pronaf) and links to rural product certificates (CPR/LCA).","outcome":"2025/26: R$516.2bn for commercial agriculture (R$414.7bn operating/commercialization, R$101.5bn investment) at 8.5-14% interest, plus R$78.2bn for family farming.","transferability":"Medium: Africa lacks the fiscal space for interest equalization at this scale, but the predictable annual credit envelope, the CPR (a tradable farm-receivable instrument) and bank lending mandates are transferable.","link":"https://www.nordinvestimentos.com.br/blog/plano-safra-2025-2026-agricultura-empresarial/","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"science-and-technology-backyards-participatory-extension","source_country":"China","theme":"Science and Technology Backyards (participatory extension)","what_happened":"China Agricultural University placed graduate students to live in villages (from Quzhou County, 2009), co-designing practices with farmers and forming quasi-cooperatives of 30-40 households.","outcome":"Leading farmers went from 67.9% to 97% of attainable yield; the county average rose from 62.8% to 79.6% (published in Nature, 2016).","transferability":"High: low-cost, university-anchored model that suits African universities and national research systems; could be combined with the youth agripreneur programs.","link":"https://penntoday.upenn.edu/research/penn-vet-researcher-helps-boost-yields-on-small-farms-in-china","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"rural-e-commerce-taobao-villages","source_country":"China","theme":"Rural e-commerce (Taobao villages)","what_happened":"Alibaba's platform plus government rural logistics and broadband drove clusters of village online shops selling farm products and crafts; Rural Taobao service centers acted as agents.","outcome":"From 20 Taobao villages in 2013 to 5,425 in 2020, with ~RMB 1 trillion in annual revenue.","transferability":"Medium: Africa's last-mile logistics, payments and connectivity usage gaps are binding constraints; social commerce (WhatsApp/TikTok) and agent networks are the more likely path.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taobao_village","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"special-economic-zones-and-infrastructure-led-industrializat","source_country":"China","theme":"Special Economic Zones and infrastructure-led industrialization (Shenzhen)","what_happened":"From 1980, SEZs offered regulatory autonomy, export processing, FDI incentives and dense infrastructure, close to Hong Kong capital and know-how.","outcome":"Shenzhen GDP went from RMB 270m (1980) to RMB 3.68 trillion (2024), more than a 10,000x increase in nominal terms.","transferability":"Medium: many African SEZs underperform. Success needs reliable power, ports, one-stop administration and anchor investors. The AU Niamey 2022 decisions and AfDB SAPZs explicitly borrow the model (see Ethiopia's Hawassa and Benin's GDIZ).","link":"https://news.cgtn.com/news/2025-08-26/Shenzhen-grows-from-a-coastal-fishing-village-into-a-global-metropolis-1G9Wt4veAWA/p.html","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"doi-moi-land-reform-and-the-rise-of-rice-exports","source_country":"Vietnam","theme":"Doi Moi land reform and the rise of rice exports","what_happened":"Doi Moi (1986), Resolution 10 (1988) gave households production rights and the right to sell output. The 1993 Land Law granted 20-50 year tradable, mortgageable land-use rights. Followed by liberalized trade and investment in Mekong Delta irrigation.","outcome":"Vietnam went from rice importer to the world's 2nd-3rd largest exporter. In 2024 it exported a record ~9 Mt worth US$5.7bn (prices above US$600/t). It is also a top coffee exporter.","transferability":"High: shows that tenure security plus market freedom can unlock smallholder output fast. Relevant to African land reform (Ethiopia, Rwanda certification) and export crop strategies.","link":"https://en.vietstock.vn/2024/12/rice-export-sets-new-record-in-2024-but-2025-expected-to-be-tough-970-596170.htm","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"drip-irrigation-and-wastewater-reuse","source_country":"Israel","theme":"Drip irrigation and wastewater reuse","what_happened":"National water pricing and allocation, commercialization of drip irrigation (Netafim and others), desalination, and near-total sewage treatment and reuse for agriculture.","outcome":"Close to 100% of sewage is reused, and ~90% of treated wastewater goes to agriculture (Mekorot).","transferability":"Medium: drip and fertigation suit high-value horticulture in water-scarce North/East/Southern Africa. Capex, power, and water-pricing reforms limit transfer to staples.","link":"https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2023/08/israels-pioneering-use-water-last-drop","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"wageningen-golden-triangle-and-greenhouse-productivity","source_country":"Netherlands","theme":"Wageningen 'golden triangle' and greenhouse productivity","what_happened":"Tight government-university-industry collaboration (Wageningen University & Research, Food Valley), controlled-environment horticulture, and logistics hubs (Rotterdam, Schiphol).","outcome":"World's 2nd-largest food exporter by value. Greenhouse tomatoes need under 4 gallons of water per kg vs 16 in open fields. Potato yields exceed 20 t/acre vs ~9 global.","transferability":"Medium: the triple-helix model and horticulture export clusters are relevant (Kenya flowers, Ethiopia/Morocco greenhouses). High-tech greenhouses are capital- and energy-intensive.","link":"https://www.nationalgeographic.com/magazine/article/holland-agriculture-sustainable-farming","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"agricultural-transformation-agency-ata-ati-delivery-unit-and","source_country":"Ethiopia (Africa, Asian-modeled)","theme":"Agricultural Transformation Agency (ATA/ATI) delivery unit and irrigated wheat","what_happened":"ATA (2010), modeled on Asian (Korea, Taiwan, Malaysia) delivery units, ran the 8028 farmer hotline, soil mapping (EthioSIS), blended fertilizer and cluster farming. From 2020 the government pushed lowland irrigated wheat with ICARDA/AfDB TAAT.","outcome":"The 8028 hotline logged ~6.5m calls and 1m+ registered users within a year (730k farmers). Wheat area in the irrigated lowlands expanded from ~20,000 ha (2020) to over 500,000 ha in two years.","transferability":"High: a peer African case of a presidential-level delivery unit (now copied in Rwanda, Nigeria, Tanzania) and IVR-based advisory at scale.","link":"https://techmoran.com/2015/06/14/ethiopias-first-agricultural-hotline-surpasses-6-million-phone-calls/","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"small-machine-mechanization-and-irrigation-service-markets","source_country":"Bangladesh","theme":"Small-machine mechanization and irrigation service markets","what_happened":"Liberalized import of Chinese power tillers and small diesel pumps (late 1980s), removal of standardization barriers, and emergence of fee-for-service rental markets.","outcome":"550,000+ power tillers prepare more than 80% of cropland. 72% of farmers use tillers and 66% use pumps through fee-for-service. Shallow tubewells grew from 93,000 (1982) to 260,000 (1990), driving near rice self-sufficiency.","transferability":"High: directly relevant to African smallholders under 2 ha. Lower import duties and standards on small machines, plus service-provider models, are low-cost policy levers.","link":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4973803","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"rice-self-sufficiency-via-bimas-and-bulog-buffer-stocks","source_country":"Indonesia","theme":"Rice self-sufficiency via BIMAS and Bulog buffer stocks","what_happened":"BIMAS 'mass guidance' bundled HYV seed, subsidized fertilizer, credit and extension. The state rehabilitated 2.5m ha of irrigation, and Bulog managed price-stabilizing buffer stocks. Renewed in 2025 with an import halt.","outcome":"Output went from ~12 Mt (1968) to 39 Mt (1985), when self-sufficiency was reached; HYV adoption rose from 50% to 85%. In 2025, output of 34.71 Mt (+13.4%) gave a 3.52 Mt surplus with no consumption imports, and Bulog stocks peaked at a record 4.2 Mt.","transferability":"Medium: shows the power of a coherent input + irrigation + price policy package. Fiscal costs and the pesticide/planthopper lesson (IPM adopted 1988) matter; Nigeria and Senegal rice strategies are analogues.","link":"https://setkab.go.id/en/food-self-sufficiency-2025-a-historic-milestone-in-indonesias-food-independence/","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"ocp-phosphate-value-addition-from-rock-to-tailored-fertilize","source_country":"Morocco (African benchmark)","theme":"OCP phosphate value addition: from rock to tailored fertilizers","what_happened":"The state-owned OCP shifted from exporting phosphate rock to fertilizers and acids, invested in integrated hubs (Jorf Lasfar), and created OCP Africa with soil mapping and customized blends for African markets.","outcome":"2024 revenue of MAD 97bn (~US$10bn), with fertilizers 69% of revenue (66% in 2023). Second phase of the investment program is under way.","transferability":"High for resource-rich states (Nigeria gas-to-urea, Ethiopia potash, Tanzania gas, Togo/Senegal phosphate). Shows that keeping value in Africa needs scale, a state champion and market development.","link":"https://www.moroccoworldnews.com/2025/03/185962/ocp-reports-solid-performance-in-2024-revenues-reach-10-billion/","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"land-grant-universities-and-cooperative-extension","source_country":"United States","theme":"Land-grant universities and cooperative extension","what_happened":"The Morrill Act (1862) funded agricultural colleges, the Hatch Act (1887) created experiment stations, and the Smith-Lever Act (1914) created the cooperative extension system linking research to farms.","outcome":"100+ land-grant institutions. Built the integrated research-education-extension model behind long-run US farm productivity growth.","transferability":"High in design: Africa's agricultural universities are rarely mandated or funded for extension. Linking universities, research institutes and county extension is a low-regret reform.","link":"https://clear.ucdavis.edu/blog/land-grant-universities-continue-shape-american-agriculture","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"cap-2023-2027-reform-green-conditionality-and-strategic-plan","source_country":"European Union","theme":"CAP 2023-2027 reform: green conditionality and strategic plans","what_happened":"Moved to national CAP Strategic Plans with performance targets. Eco-schemes take ~24% of direct payments, and payments are linked to environmental (and for the first time social/labour) conditions.","outcome":"€263bn EU funds (€307bn with national co-financing). About €98bn (~32%) goes to climate/environment actions, €8.5bn to young farmers (377,000 expected), and ~15% of farmers get risk-management support. All 28 plans were approved.","transferability":"Medium: Africa cannot match subsidy levels, but results-based national plans (as CAADP NAIPs aim to be), youth entry incentives and subsidized risk tools are instructive. EU deforestation and CBAM rules also affect African exporters.","link":"https://www.agriland.ie/farming-news/eu-commission-approves-all-28-cap-strategic-plans/","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"2024-2026-trend-generative-ai-agricultural-advisory","source_country":"Global (India/Kenya/Ethiopia/Nigeria)","theme":"2024-2026 trend: generative AI agricultural advisory","what_happened":"LLM-based advisors (Digital Green's Farmer.Chat, India's Kisan e-Mitra and AgriStack-linked AI) deliver voice/text advice in local languages, grounded in vetted extension content.","outcome":"Farmer.Chat served 15,000+ users and 300,000+ queries across Kenya (225,500 queries), India, Nigeria and Ethiopia, answering ~75% of questions. Earlier video-based extension cut cost per adoption from US$35 to US$3.50.","transferability":"High: addresses Africa's 1:2,000-1:10,000 extension ratios. Needs local-language data, content governance, and voice/IVR for low-literacy farmers.","link":"https://arxiv.org/html/2409.08916v1","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"2022-2026-trend-gene-editing-regulation-that-separates-sdn-1","source_country":"Global (Kenya/Nigeria/Ghana/Malawi)","theme":"2022-2026 trend: gene-editing regulation that separates SDN-1/2 edits from GMOs","what_happened":"Nigeria, Kenya (Feb 2022), Malawi and Ghana (Nov 2023) issued genome-editing guidelines allowing case-by-case determination that edits without foreign DNA are not regulated as GMOs. Supported by AUDA-NEPAD ABNE.","outcome":"Ghana became the 4th African country with guidelines (2023), creating faster paths for drought- and disease-resistant crops.","transferability":"High: low-cost regulatory reform. Regional harmonization via RECs would create larger markets for African breeders.","link":"https://gna.org.gh/2023/11/ghana-publishes-its-guidelines-on-genome-editing/","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"2024-2026-trend-carbon-markets-and-regenerative-agriculture-","source_country":"Global","theme":"2024-2026 trend: carbon markets and regenerative agriculture (Verra VM0042)","what_happened":"Soil-carbon methodologies (Verra VM0042) now issue credits for regenerative practices. South Africa's AgriCarbon was the first in Africa and second globally under VM0042.","outcome":"In March 2025 AgriCarbon issued 39,207 t of credits from 17,582 ha across 29 farms.","transferability":"Medium: MRV costs and price volatility make smallholder carbon hard. Aggregation, remote sensing and Article 6 frameworks are improving the economics; best as a co-benefit, not a core revenue.","link":"https://www.anthesisgroup.com/news/agricarbon-receives-verra-certified-carbon-credits-africa-first/","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"2024-2026-trend-agricultural-biologicals-biofertilizers-bios","source_country":"Global","theme":"2024-2026 trend: agricultural biologicals (biofertilizers, biostimulants, biopesticides)","what_happened":"Fast growth of microbial and biological inputs as a complement to synthetic fertilizer and pesticides, driven by soil health goals and EU pesticide restrictions.","outcome":"Global agricultural biologicals market estimated at US$15.1bn (2024), US$17.2bn (2025), projected US$43.6bn by 2032 (~14% CAGR).","transferability":"High: local production of rhizobia inoculants and biopesticides is low-capex and fits the AU Soil Health plan's 'organic and inorganic' approach. Needs registration pathways and quality control.","link":"https://fortunebusinessinsights.com/press-release/agricultural-biologicals-market-9251","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"},{"id":"2022-2024-trend-food-export-bans-and-import-dependence","source_country":"India (global shock)","theme":"2022-2024 trend: food export bans and import dependence","what_happened":"India restricted rice exports (broken rice ban Sept 2022; non-basmati white rice ban July 2023), lifting most curbs in Sept-Oct 2024.","outcome":"Indian exports fell from 21.3 to 14.3 Mt. Thai 5% prices were ~20% above pre-ban levels. Indian rice exports to Africa fell 43% (~5.3 Mt), and SSA consumers paid an estimated ~US$3.8bn extra. Indian rice was more than 60% of rice imports for 17 African countries.","transferability":"High as a lesson: argues for regional strategic reserves, AfCFTA staple trade and domestic rice/wheat productivity (the Kampala target to triple intra-African agrifood trade).","link":"https://www.ifpri.org/blog/india-lifts-export-restrictions-on-rice/","needs_review":false,"review_note":null,"last_verified":"2026-11-03"}]$atlas$::jsonb)
on conflict (id) do update set source_country = excluded.source_country, theme = excluded.theme, what_happened = excluded.what_happened, outcome = excluded.outcome, transferability = excluded.transferability, link = excluded.link, needs_review = excluded.needs_review, review_note = excluded.review_note, last_verified = excluded.last_verified;

-- meta: 1 row
insert into meta (id, data) values (1, $atlas${"as_of":"2026-11-03","lead_drivers":[{"driver":"Sustained public investment in R&D and extension, with institutional autonomy","examples":"Brazil's Embrapa (TFP 3.08%/yr, 80% of output growth), US land-grant/Hatch/Smith-Lever system, China's Science and Technology Backyards, Netherlands WUR. Contrast: ~60% of SSA countries spend under 0.5% of AgGDP (ASTI).","explanation":"Leaders fund research at or above 1% of AgGDP over decades through autonomous, performance-managed institutions linked to extension. Productivity (TFP), not land expansion, then drives growth."},{"driver":"Bundled input packages delivered at scale (seed + fertilizer + water + credit)","examples":"India Green Revolution (Punjab), Indonesia BIMAS (12 to 39 Mt rice, 1968-85), Ethiopia irrigated wheat clusters, AU Fertilizer Action Plan targeting 54 kg/ha.","explanation":"Single interventions fail; Green Revolutions packaged HYVs with assured water, affordable fertilizer, credit and advisory, first in high-potential zones."},{"driver":"Secure, tradable land rights and market freedom for smallholders","examples":"Vietnam Resolution 10 (1988) and the 1993 Land Law took it from rice importer to a 9 Mt exporter. China's household responsibility system. Africa: only ~10% of rural land registered; Rwanda and Ethiopia certification as African analogues.","explanation":"When farmers own output and can lease, mortgage and invest in land, supply responds quickly and credit can flow."},{"driver":"Infrastructure first: water, power, roads and storage","examples":"Asia has ~37% of cropland irrigated vs ~6% in Africa. Bangladesh shallow tubewells. China SEZ/infrastructure (Shenzhen GDP 10,000x). Mission 300 and PIDA as African responses.","explanation":"Irrigation, reliable electricity and rural roads lower unit costs and make inputs, mechanization, cold chains and processing viable."},{"driver":"Digital public infrastructure and farmer registries","examples":"India UPI (24bn transactions/month), PM-KISAN (93m farmers by DBT), AgriStack (110m farmer IDs), e-NAM. Africa leads in mobile money but lacks interoperable registries.","explanation":"Open ID, payments and registries cut the cost of reaching each farmer with subsidies, credit, insurance and advice, and they create the data for targeting."},{"driver":"Predictable, long-horizon policy and delivery units with political ownership","examples":"Brazil annual Plano Safra, EU CAP strategic plans, Ethiopia ATA (Asian delivery-unit model), Dakar 2 presidential delivery councils. Contrast: no country on track in the Malabo 4th Biennial Review.","explanation":"Countries that lead keep agricultural and industrial policy stable for decades and run them through empowered delivery units with clear metrics and heads-of-state attention."},{"driver":"Value addition anchored by industrial champions and export discipline","examples":"OCP Morocco (69% of ~US$10bn revenue from fertilizers, not rock), Vietnam cashew/coffee processing, Shenzhen SEZ. Contrast: ~75% of West African cocoa and ~95% of cashew exported raw.","explanation":"Leaders move up the value chain with scaled champions, export-processing zones and standards, rather than shipping raw commodities."},{"driver":"Market integration and openness to regional and global trade","examples":"EU single market, China rural e-commerce (5,425 Taobao villages), India e-NAM. Africa: intra-African trade ~15%; India's rice ban cost SSA consumers ~US$3.8bn; AfCFTA and regional seed harmonization are the fix.","explanation":"Large integrated markets reward scale in seed, processing and logistics, and reduce exposure to export bans by single suppliers."}],"market_note":{"sources":["https://www.briter.co/insights/reports/state-of-agtech-investment-in-africa-2025","https://techeconomy.ng/african-agtech-funding-decline-2025-capital-shift-agbase-report/","https://techcabal.com/2026/01/29/agritech-funding-in-africa-falls-to-168-million/","https://agsafari.beehiiv.com/p/african-agtechs-raised-169-5-million-in-2025","https://allafrica.com/stories/202601250064.html"],"summary":"Briter's State of Agtech Investment in Africa 2025 (AgBase) puts African agtech funding at just under $170M in 2025, about 20% below the roughly $200M of 2024. Both funding and deal count fell in the same year for the first time, and equity dropped from about $328M in 2022 to about $80M, so debt, hybrid instruments and grants made up most of the capital. Briter data cited by TechCabal gives $168.1M across 135 deals (2024: $206.9M, 146 deals), against the peak of $483M in 2022. Africa: The Big Deal counts $169.45M across 87 deals (>$100K), about 5% of African tech funding. Capital is spreading out geographically: Kenya's share fell from over 50% in 2023-24 to about 25%. Egypt ($52.4M), Nigeria ($29.6M), South Africa ($28.9M) and Tunisia ($24.2M) led in 2025, and the largest deals were Tagaddod ($26M), nextProtein ($20.7M), Nile ($11.3M), Koolboks ($11.2M), Pula ($10.4M) and SwiftVEE ($10.1M). Across all African tech, Partech counts $4.1B in 2025 (+25%), with debt at 41% of capital (31% in 2024). This mirrors agtech's turn toward infrastructure-backed debt, as in d.light's $300M receivables deal and ThriveAgric's N5.3bn commercial paper, while 2024-25 also brought restructurings and failures (Gro, iProcure, Lori's down round, Twiga)."},"score_method":{"version":"2026-11-09","pillars":{"productivity":["Cereal yield t/ha (FAO via OWID, 2024)","Ag value added per worker, constant US$ (World Bank WDI via OWID, 2025)"],"inputs_water":["Fertilizer kg/ha of cropland (FAO via OWID, 2023)","Irrigated area as % of cropland (CIA irrigated land 2012-2020 / FAO cropland 2023; Rwanda, Kenya and Ghana from national figures in the deep dives)"],"policy":["CAADP 4th Biennial Review score vs 9.29 benchmark","Ag share of public spending vs 10% target (SDG 2.a.1 orientation index x ag % of GDP, 2023)"],"infrastructure":["Electricity access %","Internet users %"],"finance":["Adults with a bank or mobile-money account % (Global Findex, 2024)"]},"rule":"Indicators are ranked 0-100 against the other African countries (CAADP and spending scored against their targets); a pillar averages its indicators; the composite averages pillars and needs at least 4 of 5."}}$atlas$::jsonb)
on conflict (id) do update set data = excluded.data;

commit;
